   <section xml:id=3D"cll_chapter4-section2">
     <title>2. cmavo</title>
     <para>The first group of Lojban words discussed in this chapter are th=
e cmavo. They are the structure words that hold the Lojban language togethe=
r. They often have no semantic meaning in themselves, though they may affec=
t the semantics of brivla to which they are attached. The cmavo include the=
 equivalent of English articles, conjunctions, prepositions, numbers, and p=
unctuation marks. There are over a hundred subcategories of cmavo, known as=
=20
     <quote>selma'o</quote>, each having a specifically defined grammatical=
 usage. The various selma'o are discussed throughout=20
     <xref linkend=3D"cll_chapter5" />to=20
     <xref linkend=3D"cll_chapter19" />and summarized in=20
     <xref linkend=3D"cll_chapter20" />.</para>
     <para>Standard cmavo occur in four forms defined by their word structu=
re. Here are some examples of the various forms:</para>
-    <programlisting xml:space=3D"preserve">
